findings.body:
The setter was changed from `value <= 0` to `value < 0`, so 0 passes validation. `FileLoggerProvider` only prunes when `_maxRetainedFiles > 0`, so a stored 0 means files are never deleted. The XML doc just added says the value is "strictly positive", and the exception text still says "must be positive". Nothing in the PR explains why the guard was relaxed, and no tests cover this boundary.

::diff{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/AzureFileLoggerOptions.cs" lines="48-58"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a runtime behavior change inside a documentation PR. Setting `RetainedFileCountLimit = 0` used to throw. Now it silently turns off retention, and log directories can grow without limit.

If the intent was to allow 0 as "no limit", the doc and API should say so and the provider should be explicit about it. Otherwise, the guard should go back to `<= 0`. Either way, add a boundary test for 0 and for -1 on this setter.

The setter now uses `value <= 0`, so 0 throws. This matches `BlockingCollection`, which requires a positive bound, so the change itself looks right. However, the exception text still says "must be non-negative", which now contradicts the check. The doc was also rewritten without noting that 0 is no longer accepted, and the change is not mentioned in the PR description.

::diff{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/BatchingLoggerOptions.cs" lines="42-53"}

::suggestion{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/BatchingLoggerOptions.cs" lines="47-49"}
if (value <= 0)
{
    throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(value), $"{nameof(BackgroundQueueSize)} must be positive.");
}

Also note in the doc that the value must be greater than zero, or null for no limit.

Neither the `RetainedFileCountLimit` setter nor the `BackgroundQueueSize` setter has a test that checks the boundary value. `FileLoggerTests` and `BatchingLoggerProviderTests` only exercise valid values. A boundary test would have caught the regression in the first finding. Because the PR is mostly doc edits, these two behavior changes are easy to miss in review.

::callout{type="info"}
Add tests in `src/Logging.AzureAppServices/test/` for `RetainedFileCountLimit` at 0, 1, and -1, and for `BackgroundQueueSize` at 0, 1, and -1. Each should assert that the setter throws `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` for the invalid values and accepts the valid ones.

The opening brace was moved onto the signature line, which is not the Allman style used everywhere else in this file and codebase. It looks like an accidental edit in a doc-focused PR.

